Let $h$ be the height of the mosaic in inches, and $l$ be the length of the mosaic in inches. The area of the mosaic is $A = h \times l$ square inches.
It takes 12 glass chips to make every square inch of the mosaic. So the number of chips required to make the mosaic is $12 \times A = 12 \times h \times l$.
A bag of glass chips holds 72 chips. Milo has 2 bags of glass chips, so he has $2 \times 72 = 144$ chips in total.
The number of chips required is $12 \times h \times l$, so we have $12 \times h \times l = 144$.
Milo wants his mosaic to be three inches tall, so $h = 3$.
Substituting $h=3$ into the equation, we get $12 \times 3 \times l = 144$.
$36l = 144$.
Dividing both sides by 36, we get $l = \frac{144}{36} = 4$.
So he can make the mosaic 4 inches long.

